============================================================
DEPENDENCY INVERSION PRINCIPLE (DIP) – THEORY POINTS
============================================================

1️⃣ Definition (Formal)
----------------------
High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules.
Both should depend on abstractions.

Abstractions should not depend on details.
Details should depend on abstractions.


2️⃣ Layman Definition ⭐
----------------------
Do not depend on a specific class.
Depend on an interface so that implementations can change
without breaking your code.


3️⃣ What Is a High-Level Module?
-------------------------------
• Contains business logic
• Controls application flow
• Example: OrderService, PaymentService


4️⃣ What Is a Low-Level Module?
------------------------------
• Performs technical work
• Sends emails, SMS, DB operations, APIs
• Example: EmailService, WhatsAppService


5️⃣ Why DIP Exists
-----------------
• Reduce tight coupling
• Make code flexible
• Support future changes
• Improve testability
• Enable clean architecture


6️⃣ Common DIP Violations
------------------------
• Using `new` keyword inside business logic
• Direct dependency on concrete classes
• Hardcoding implementations
• No abstraction layer


7️⃣ How DIP Fixes the Problem
-----------------------------
• Introduce interfaces
• High-level modules depend on interfaces
• Low-level modules implement interfaces
• Dependency is injected (Constructor / Setter / DI)


8️⃣ DIP + Dependency Injection (DI)
----------------------------------
DIP is a DESIGN PRINCIPLE
DI is a TECHNIQUE to implement DIP

Example:
• Constructor Injection
• Setter Injection
• Hilt / Dagger / Koin


9️⃣ Real-World Analogy
---------------------
Switch board:
• Old way ❌: Fixed wiring for each appliance
• DIP way ✅: Plug any appliance into socket

Socket = Interface
Appliance = Implementation


🔟 DIP in Android Development
-----------------------------
• Repository pattern
• ViewModel depends on interface, not DB
• Retrofit via interfaces
• UseCase pattern
• Hilt / Dagger dependency injection


🎯 Interview One-Liners
----------------------
• “DIP says depend on abstractions, not implementations.”
• “High-level logic should not depend on low-level details.”
• “DIP enables loose coupling and flexibility.”
